`Anchor` is unchanged (`packages/frontend/selection.ts`). It is a half-open range over the *visible* message list of one thread, and it is deliberately not a DOM `Range`: the browser collapses the live selection as soon as the user clicks the right pane, whereas offsets survive, serialize, and stay valid as a message streams in (the transcript is append-only).
```ts
/** `msg` indexes a thread's visible messages; `offset` is a character offset
 * into that message's text. */
export type Point = { msg: number; offset: number };
/** `start` precedes `end` in document order; equal points mean no selection. */
export type Anchor = { start: Point; end: Point };
```
An `Anchor` is meaningful only relative to the thread it was taken from, which is why `Mark` pairs one with a `ThreadId` and why `Origin` stores it next to its parent.
`packages/frontend/selection.ts` gains the segment split:
```ts
export type Mark = { anchor: Anchor; thread: ThreadId };
export type Segment = {
  start: number;
  end: number;
  thread: ThreadId | null; // null = the live, uncommitted selection
};
/** Splits message `i` into plain and marked runs. */
